  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Sunday...\n\nThere is a large amount of low stratus/fog along the western \nfoothills, and web cameras at noon continue to show very poor \nvisibility at Bozeman Pass and Red Lodge. High res models show the\ngreatest potential for fog thru tonight near western foothills \nand this seems accurate given the easterly winds and snow cover, \nwith moisture being aided by a bit of melting today. So expect the\ncombination of stratus and fog to persist through the remainder \nof the day and into Monday morning. Have issued a Dense Fog \nAdvisory for all western foothills zones thru 17z Monday. \nConditions will improve thru the course of the day tomorrow as \nwinds become westerly. Fog potential will shift to the east and be\nmuch lower Monday night as downslope gradients develop.\n\nSatellite imagery shows flat ridge over MT with a weak shortwave\nlifting out of southern ID and a parent low off the PacNW coast.\nWeak ascent glancing our west this afternoon may bring a few light\nsnow showers/flurries to western mountains & foothills. Another \nweak wave off the CA coast will move inland and track thru mainly\nthe central & southern Rockies Monday, but this should be enough \nfor scattered light snow showers over southern mountains tomorrow.\nLower elevations will stay dry. Temperatures will remain cooler \nthan normal today & Monday, with tomorrow being a bit warmer as \nlee side surface high drifts eastward and gradients relax. \n\nThe weather regime changes to downsloping/warmer/windier late \nMonday night & Tuesday, and this will continue through the week.\nRegarding wind, mid level flow will be fairly weak but surface\ngradient and instability support increased gap flow by early \nTuesday and again late Tuesday night into Wednesday. Gusts may\napproach 50 mph during both of these periods (40% chance). The \ngreater wind potential for not only the gap locations but across \nthe western foothills is Thursday into Friday. Increasing mid \nlevel winds (near 50 knots at 700mb) and tightening surface \ngradients (15-20mb from IDA-LWT per the GFS/EC) suggest a high \nprobability of 50+ mph gusts (80-90%) and a good chance of 60+ mph\ngusts (50-70% chance) at the usual locations. Into Friday as the \nsurface low tracks east and cold advection develops, gusty/mixed \nW-NW winds spread across lower elevations. There is considerable \nensemble spread in the strength of this wave and magnitude of \npost-frontal winds, but this is a time period to keep an eye on. \nThere is also a low chance of lower elevation rain showers, but it\nis much more likely that there is a period of orographic mountain\nsnow on W-NW aspects.\n\nWhereas yesterday's ensembles suggested Saturday would be the\nwarmest day of the week, it now looks as if a quicker trend to\nlower heights, NW flow and some cooling arrive by Friday and\ncontinue into the weekend. The Pacific flow should keep temps \nwarmer than normal, but highs are expected to be in the 40s to mid\n50s rather than 50s to lower 60s next weekend. There is also a \nrisk of additional shortwaves into the weekend, so generally \nspeaking the weather pattern is looking more unsettled than it did\nbefore.\n\nWe are entering a holiday week and the days leading up to \nThanksgiving look dry, and travel conditions around the region \nshould be good. Thanksgiving itself is looking windy along the \nfoothills and this will be something to keep in mind. Passes could\nbe impacted by snow showers at times Thursday night thru the \nweekend.\n\nJKL",
